⏺ Lighthouses have guided sailors for thousands of years.
-- screen
  Their bright beams warn ships about rocks, shoals, and
  dangerous coastlines. Early towers burned wood or coal,
  while later lenses threw light many miles across the
  water. Today most are automated, controlled by computers
  and monitored from distant stations. Many have become
  museums or tourist attractions, drawing visitors who climb
  the spiral stairs for the view. Though fewer are needed
  for navigation now, they remain symbols of safety,
  patience, and human determination against the sea.

